				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/chazzk" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1203158); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1203158&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608973930" alt="ChazzK" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1203158)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/chazzk" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1203158); return false;" >ChazzK</a></span> <span class="odate time_1379534865 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">18 Sep 2013 20:07</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1854464">
									

<p>While I don't like it enough to upvote (sincerely don't like it enough to downvote, either) I think there's something else being missed. It's not the entire cosmos that trembles in fear; it's <em>this particular group of aliens</em>. After all, they showed up and probably treated us as a curiosity; maybe it was even the first time they got to watch a non-engineered intelligent race develop from the ground up. Then, a shadow-beast started to stalk the wilderness. A generation later, someone &quot;dug up&quot; a rock that inverted the colors of everything within a 15 foot radius. Someone gets born with the ability to drive people into death-by-dehydration levels of apathy by licking them. What started as a simple observation slowly turned into a horror show, and it just keeps getting worse.</p>
<p>To put it in another perspective: if the average person heard &quot;SCP-106 has broken containment&quot;, they'd be confused, at worst mildly afraid because words like &quot;broken containment&quot; are never good, maybe even slightly annoyed by the speaker. But those in the know would probably PANIC. 99.999% of the universe don't give a flippy farewell to the fate of &quot;humans&quot;&#8230; That 0.001% shake at the potential.</p>

							</div>

								
					
